{{Nihongo|'''Okami'''|大紙|Ōkami}}; officially {{Nihongo|'''Okami Metropolis'''|大紙都|Ōkami-to}}, is the [[capital of Okaiken]] and the most populous metropolitan area in the nation. With a population of over 8.2 million in its 18 special wards and over 36.9 million in the Greater Okami metropolitan area, it is the political, economic, and cultural heart of the country. Okami serves as the seat of the [[Government of Okaiken|Sanese government]], the [[Kokyo|Imperial Palace]], and the [[Imperial House of Okaiken|Sanese Imperial Family]].

Located in the [[Tokami region]] on the northwestern side of the island [[Harujima]], Okami Metropolis was formed in 1973 from the merger of the former {{Nihongo|Okami Prefecture|大紙府|Okami-fu}} and {{Nihongo|[[Okami City]]|大紙市|Okami-shi}}. The metropolis administers the 18 [[special wards of Okami]] (each governed as an individual city), which cover the area that was historically the city of Okami, as well as 20 additional municipalities in the western part of the prefecture and the outlying [[Inland Mitoyagi Islands]] island chain.

As a [[global city]], Okami has been described as one of the three "command centers" for the [[world economy]], alongside [[New Valencia]] and [[Brisa]]. The city is considered an [[wikipedia:global city|alpha+ world city]], listed by the [[GaWC]]'s 2008 inventory and ranked fourth among global cities by ''[[J.T. Kearney]]'''s 2012 [[wikipedia:Global city#Global Cities Index|Global Cities Index]]. In 2010, Okami was named the [[List of most expensive cities|second most expensive city]] for expatriate employees, according to the [[w:Mercer (consulting firm)|Mercer]] and [[Economist Intelligence Unit]] cost-of-living surveys. The [[Adonia Guide]] has awarded Okami by far the most Rosalin stars of any city in the world. The city hosts 47 of the [[Global 500]] companies, the highest amount of any city globally, and serves as the headquarters for numerous multinational corporations and financial institutions.

Okami's significance extends beyond economics; as the national capital, it houses all branches of the Sanese government, including the [[National Diet Building]], the official residence of the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]], and the [[Supreme Court of Okaiken|Supreme Court]]. The city's unique history as the political center of the [[Honda Shogunate]] for over 250 years, followed by its modern role as the capital of a constitutional monarchy, has shaped it into a metropolis where ancient temples and shrines stand alongside cutting-edge skyscrapers and the world's most advanced urban infrastructure.

==Etymology==
Okami was originally known as Oka-mi (岡海), a kanji compound of 岡 (''oka'', 'hill, ridge') and 海 (''mi'', 'sea, inlet'). The name, which roughly translates to "ridge in the sea," was a direct reference to the original settlement's geography: a small fishing hamlet situated on a slight elevation amidst the sprawling, marshy delta of the then Kumagawa and Kashi rivers.

When the [[Honda clan]] terraformed the delta and manipulated the relocation of the Imperial court to the city in 1610, the spelling was altered by the Shogunate to Ōkami (大神; from 大 ''ō'' 'great', and 神 ''kami'' 'deity' or 'spirit'). This profound elevation in the name's meaning was intended to reflect the city's new status as the sacred and political center of the archipelago. However, utilizing the character for "god" for a worldly city was frequently considered taboo by the traditional religious establishment in [[Mito]]. Consequently, secular merchants and commoners often used alternative phonetic spellings in written correspondence, most notably 大上 (Ōkami, 'Great Above') or 大水 (Ōmizu, 'Great Water').

During the [[Saito Restoration]] in 1871, the government sought to rapidly modernize the nation, diminish the religious overtones of the former Shogunate, and centralize administration. The kanji for the city was officially standardized to its current form: Okami (大紙; from 大 ''ō'' 'great/big', and 紙 ''kami'' 'paper'). 

The selection of "Big Paper" was highly deliberate. Practically, it served as a nod to the city's historic artisan districts along the Garyū River, which had long utilized the uniquely clear, turquoise waters of the delta to produce the nation's highest-quality ''washi'' paper. Symbolically, however, it represented Okami's new identity as the bureaucratic nerve center of a modernizing empire—the exact place where the new constitution, modern laws, imperial decrees, and the new national currency were printed and disseminated. 

During the early Republic Era, following the [[Quiet Revolution]], the Federal Republic of Okaiken temporarily abolished the name. In 1971, the city was renamed to Shinkyō (新京, 'New Capital') in an attempt to sever all linguistic ties to the Imperial past, following the East Fosian tradition of including the word for capital (京) in the name. It was not until the conservative [[Jutsu Restoration]] in 2011 that a national referendum successfully reverted the city's official name back to the historic Okami (大紙).

==History==
{{Main|History of Okami}}

===Prehistory to the Sengoku period===
[[File:Morinomiya Shell Mounds Excavation.png|thumb|left|Excavated shell mounds at the Morinomiya site, indicating early coastal settlement.]]
Some of the earliest signs of habitation in the area of Okami were found at the Morinomiya remains (森の宮遺跡 Morinomiya iseki), with its shell mounds, including sea oysters and buried human skeletons dating back to the 5th–6th centuries BC. It is believed that what is today the Uehonmachi area consisted of peninsular land, with an inland sea to the east. During the Miyagi period, permanent habitation on the plains grew as rice farming became popular. However, habitation at Okami remained sparse; it was a little-known small fishing village by the late Sengoku Period.

Archaeological evidence suggests that the area was originally inhabited by the proto-Atsu peoples before their displacement by Yoroi settlers from the south. The shell middens at Morinomiya indicate a sophisticated coastal culture that relied heavily on marine resources, with evidence of trade connections extending as far as the northern coast of Harujima. The area's strategic position at the mouth of the Kumagawa and Kashi rivers made it a natural crossing point for trade between the interior highlands and the coastal waterways.

During the [[Asama period]] (710–794), the area was administratively part of the greater Settsu Province, though it remained a peripheral region of little political significance. The earliest written records mentioning the settlement date from 743, when a minor customs outpost was established to monitor river traffic. This outpost, staffed by a handful of imperial officials, represented the only governmental presence in the area for centuries.

===Sengoku period===
[[File:Terumasa Kofukuji screen.png|thumb|left|A traditional screen painting depicting the siege of Kofukuji temple in 1592.]]
During the Mid-Sengoku Period, Okami was governed by the [[Kamigata clan]], a small, poor clan with a direct tutelage to the [[Ogasawara clan]], which was located 75 kilometres north of the hamlet in Iwawada. Okami was still a small village with a poorly defended castle at the banks of the Kumagawa and Kashi rivers. Surrounded by both salt and freshwater marshes, the area was deemed difficult for land surveying and rice farming. By the beginning of the Seto-Tosa period, the clan surrendered its territories to the Ogasawara clan, citing difficult conditions to properly govern the area. Ogasawara Terumasa then launched a series of civil engineering projects into the river, such as irrigation canals and defensive embankments that are still being used by the government today. 

As planned during his regional consolidation, [[Ogasawara Terumasa]] stayed in Okami's Kofukuji temple. On 21 April 1592, the same day that Terumasa perished in the infamous Kofukuji coup, the Takigawa army led by Maeda Toshiie successfully repelled a small contingent of Ogasawara soldiers and immediately notified [[Honda Shigetada]] about the betrayal of [[Takigawa Kazumasu]]. The Honda clan quickly regrouped and proceeded to attack [[Otomo Castle]] on 3 March 1592, successfully freeing the children and wives of the Honda clan and causing the total destruction of the fortress. Honda Shigetada then returned to Nagashino and made amends with Takigawa Kazumasu, who now controlled the majority of the Ogasawara clan's holdings in Harujima. Takigawa Kazumasu restored Honda Shigetada's rice allocation to 125,000 koku and allowed his vassals to return to his service. 

Eventually, the territory passed through several hands, such as the Hisamichi and the Kishi, before the Honda clan was officially moved to the area in exchange for their territory in Yagikoshu. 

===Honda Period and the Great Delta Reclamation===
[[File:Okami Honda Shigetomo.png|thumb|right|Honda Shigetomo reviewing the architectural plans for the new capital, with the early construction of Okami Castle visible in the background.]]
Even before their rise to absolute national power, the [[Honda clan]] had been seeking a new location to relocate their primary stronghold, finding their former territories in the northern mountains insufficient for large-scale administration and maritime trade. They ultimately selected the estuarine river delta that would become Okami. 

Historical accounts describe the early marshland as sweltering and inhospitable, though the waters were noted for their surprisingly clear, turquoise hue. Surveying the new territory proved difficult; a well-known historical anecdote records that [[Honda Shigetomo]] was abruptly thrown into the turquoise marsh when his flat-bottomed surveying boat struck a submerged rock. 

Despite the difficult environment, the clan initiated unprecedented land reclamation projects. To accelerate the development of the new capital, Shigetomo famously took a hands-on approach to the city's construction. Contemporary records note that the clan leader frequently worked alongside common laborers, directly participating in the paving of roads and the raising of earthen embankments. 

[[File:Okami Delta Edo Map.png|thumb|left|An intricate 17th-century map detailing the "Dragon's Claw" river diversion project that formed the five main islands of Okami.]]
To tame the unpredictable flooding of the Kumagawa and Kashi rivers and create a defensible urban core, Shigetomo commissioned a monumental hydro-engineering initiative known as the "Dragon's Claw Division." Under the brilliant direction of Chief Surveyor Takebe Rinzō and Master of Waterworks Hayashi Tadamasa, tens of thousands of conscripted laborers were mobilized. 

To finance and execute this impossible scale of construction, the Honda Shogunate instituted the ''tetsudai-bushin'' (public works burden) system. Defeated or subordinate domain lords were explicitly ordered to supply materials and labor, deliberately draining their wealth to prevent future rebellions. The Takigawa clan of [[Harujima]] was tasked with excavating the sprawling outer moats and the intricate canal networks. Meanwhile, the Hisamichi and Kishi clans of the northern provinces were forced to fell and transport thousands of massive cypress logs down the coast to build the castle's main keep and the surrounding palisades.

The engineering process was executed in three methodical steps:
* '''Diversion:''' The massive flow of the Kumagawa River was partially dammed upstream, and its waters were forcibly redirected into four newly dredged arterial canals (the Kōryū, Garyū, Sōryū, and Kairyū).
* '''Elevation:''' The millions of tons of rich sediment excavated from these new channels were systematically piled onto the unstable marshland, raising the ground level safely above the region's devastating King Tides. 
* '''Stabilization:''' Massive retaining walls made of interlocking granite blocks were constructed along the new shorelines, locking the silt in place and serving as the foundational bases for merchant docks and defensive turrets.

This monumental feat of terraforming transformed the singular, inhospitable marsh into a highly organized, defensible archipelago of five primary landmasses, which dictate the urban geography and cultural genesis of Okami to this day:

* '''Tenkyō-jima''' (天鏡島 - "Heavenly Mirror Island"): The imperial and political heartland. This heavily fortified northernmost island is where Okami Castle was constructed. Today, it houses Okami Chūō, the National Diet, and the elite administrative districts.
* '''Mugishima''' (麦島 - "Barley Island"): The traditional merchant district. It is split by the flowing waters of the Garyū and Sōryū rivers, which historically served as the primary logistical arteries for rice and timber barges. The historic merchant stalls here evolved into the city's modern high-end commercial and culinary center.
* '''Nagashima''' (長島 - "Long Island"): The civic center, nestled tightly between the same "Inner Claw" rivers as Mugishima. It originally housed the sprawling, manicured estates of the Shogun's most trusted retainers.
* '''Tamashima''' (玉島 - "Jewel Island"): Separated from the northern islands by the wide and turbulent Kōryū River. Originally serving as the muddy, unregulated encampment for the tens of thousands of conscripted laborers building the city, Tamashima naturally evolved into a chaotic entertainment zone filled with teahouses, gambling dens, and kabuki theaters. This island is the direct historical genesis of Okami's modern, world-famous Neon District and "Chaotic Downtown."
* '''Ryūbi-jima''' (竜尾島 - "Dragon Tail Island"): The final landmass formed by the Kairyū river before the delta meets the open waters of the Bay. It historically served as the outer naval defense line. The eastern docks of Ryūbi-jima became the primary gateway for continental trade, laying the exact geographic groundwork for the modern Okami Industrial Zone and the heavy shipyards of Tatara Ward.

[[File:Old plans Okami.png|thumb|left|The original plans for Okami castle, which including redirecting the Okami River into distinct rivers.]]

The political status of Okami was formalized when the Honda clan assumed control over the archipelago in 1605. Following his decisive victory at the Battle of Tone, Shigetomo strategically reduced his direct, personal domain from 580,000 koku to 200,000 koku. This redistribution of wealth and land was used to reward his retainers and solidify their absolute loyalty, effectively establishing the foundation of the Honda Shogunate.

The city's prestige was further elevated in 1610 under a unique dual-capital system. Honda Shigetada, having ascended to the throne under the name Emperor Shirakawa, manipulated court dynamics to relocate the administrative and political apparatus of the Imperial institution from its traditional seat in [[Mito]] to Okami. However, not all Imperial functions were transferred. The sacred ancestral shrines and the traditional, highly secretive ritualistic functions of the Imperial family remained strictly in Mito. 

To meet the immense administrative demands of the relocated political court, Okami Castle underwent significant expansion into a sprawling palace complex. This concentration of military and political power triggered an unprecedented demographic explosion, turning a fishing community of 100 people into a booming metropolis of 200,000. 

The division of Imperial duties between Okami and Mito persisted for over 250 years. It was not until the Late Saito Restoration in the 1870s that the remaining ancestral shrines and all religious functions of the Imperial household were permanently consolidated in Okami, finally making the delta city the absolute and sole capital of the empire in every capacity.

====The Honda Shogunate Capital (1601–1871)====
During the two and a half centuries of Honda rule, Okami developed into one of the largest and most sophisticated cities in the pre-modern world. The population grew from approximately 200,000 in 1610 to over 1.2 million by the mid-19th century, making it one of the most populous urban centers on the planet during that era.

The city's growth was carefully managed by the shogunal administration. A system of strict zoning separated the warrior estates (''bukeyashiki'') of Nagashima from the merchant quarters (''chōnin-machi'') of Mugishima and the entertainment districts of Tamashima. The shogunate established Okami as the terminus of the major highways connecting the capital to the provinces, including the Tōkaidō coastal road and the Nakasendō mountain route. The city became the undisputed center of Sanese culture, giving birth to the distinctive ''Okami-mono'' aesthetic that would define the period's art, literature, and theater.

The shogunal capital also became famous for its distinctive "Hyakunin Isshu" system of city governance, in which one hundred appointed ward elders (''machi-doshiyori'') managed local affairs under the supervision of the city magistrate (''machi-bugyō''). This system, while autocratic by modern standards, provided remarkable stability and allowed for the development of sophisticated urban infrastructure, including fire brigades, water supply systems, and waste management networks that were among the most advanced in the world at the time.

===Saito Restoration===
[[File:Katsu Kaishu Surrender.png|thumb|left|Statesman Katsu Kaishū negotiating the bloodless surrender of Okami Castle to Imperial forces in 1871.]]
By the late 19th century, the political stability of the Honda Shogunate had deeply eroded, leading to a nationwide push to restore direct Imperial rule. In 1871, during the climax of the [[Saito Restoration]], Imperial loyalist forces marched on Okami. 

To spare the densely populated city and its historic castle from the devastation of modern artillery, the visionary statesman [[Katsu Kaishū]] negotiated the bloodless surrender of Okami Castle to the Imperial army. This masterful diplomatic stroke officially ended centuries of Honda Shogunate rule and transitioned Okami into a modern Imperial capital under Emperor Saito, kicking off a period of rapid Westernization, industrialization, and infrastructural growth.

The Saito Restoration brought profound changes to the city. The feudal domains were abolished and replaced with a modern system of prefectures. Okami Castle, formerly the seat of shogunal power, was renamed the Imperial Palace and became the primary residence of the Imperial family. The city's streets were widened to accommodate modern transportation, gas lighting was installed in major thoroughfares, and the first rail lines were constructed to connect the capital with the emerging industrial centers.

In 1872, the first railway line in Okaiken opened between Okami and neighboring [[Tokuyama]], marking the beginning of the nation's modern transportation era. By 1890, the city's population had reached 1.5 million, and it had become the undisputed center of the nation's emerging industrial economy.

===Great Adonian War===
[[File:Okami Firebombing Devastation 1952.png|thumb|right|The charred ruins of central Okami following the devastating incendiary air raids of the Great Adonian War.]]
During the Great Adonian War in the mid-20th century, Okami's status as the industrial and logistical heart of the empire made it a primary military target. The city was subjected to relentless and devastating aerial bombardment by Allied forces. The bombing raids caused massive civilian casualties, flattened the industrial sectors, and burned vast swaths of the historic wooden city to ash. By the time of the nation's surrender, Okami was a devastated shell of its former glory, requiring a monumental, decades-long rebuilding effort.

The most devastating attack came on the night of March 9–10, 1951, when a force of over 300 enemy bombers dropped incendiary devices on the densely populated residential districts of eastern Okami. The resulting firestorm destroyed over 40% of the city's urban area and killed an estimated 80,000 civilians in a single night. The historic districts of Tamashima and the older merchant quarters of Mugishima were largely reduced to ashes, though the Imperial Palace and the government district on Tenkyō-jima sustained relatively less damage.

===Modern Okami===
[[File:Okami Skyline Tama.png|thumb|right|The hyper-dense skyline of modern Tama Island, which absorbed much of the city's post-war commercial growth.]]
[[File:Harujima Shinkansen 1960.jpg|thumb|left|The inaugural Harujima Shinkansen arrives at Okami Central Station, completely transforming national transit ahead of the 1960 Adonian Games.]]
After the [[Great Adonian War]], the government announced a sweeping urban plan to decrease the population density around the ruined old castle town and distribute it to newer satellite districts such as Kanda, Shinbashi, and Jomyou-in in Eastern Okami, as well as the western areas of Tama and Rinkan. As a result, the current density of the historic castle district is much lower than the surrounding wards, while Shinbashi rapidly evolved into one of the world's densest and most economically powerful urban areas.

To support these new town centres, a new east-west commuter rail line called the Tama-Shinjo Line was built in 1957 to complement the Okami Loop Line and the Okaiken Railways corridor at Kanda. Private railway companies such as the [[Fukukyu Railway]] and the [[Akitetsu Railway]] extended their commuter services deep into the suburbs, allowing the city to take on a staggering rate of rapid growth in the 1960s. During this post-war economic miracle, Okami experienced a population and economic explosion that rivalled the combined growth of the largest metropolitan areas in the hemisphere.

A major milestone in Okami's rebirth was the opening of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] in 1959, completed just in time for Okami to host the [[1960 Adonian Games]]. The global sporting event showcased the city's miraculous recovery and its new cutting-edge infrastructure to the world.

By 1968, the city had quadrupled in size, and its population density had returned to pre-war levels. To manage this hyper-growth, the Okami Metropolitan Government and Council were created to streamline the approval of massive infrastructure projects dotting the city. Two new north-south lines of the Okaiken Railways (the Sanshi Line and the Kanda-Toranomon Line) were constructed. Nagashima was zoned specifically for large-scale skyscraper development, while Kanda and Tama were allocated for mid-rise commercial buildings. New restrictions and heritage guidelines were established for the old castle town to control population density and preserve the city's historical character. 

By 1973, major reconstruction work on the ruined [[Okami Castle]] had begun, and five new private railway lines were opened to handle the surging commuter traffic. The metropolitan subway system was finally extended in 1975 with the completion of the [[Asahi line]]. In the next two years, four more subway lines were opened to the public. New metropolitan centres at Okami's surrounding areas were built and seamlessly connected by either private lines or the Okaiken Railways Group. 

====The 1960 Adonian Games====
Okami's selection as host of the 1960 Adonian Games represented a watershed moment in the city's post-war recovery. The games served as a powerful symbol of Okaiken's re-emergence onto the world stage and provided the impetus for massive infrastructure investment. The [[Harujima Shinkansen]], opened in 1959 specifically for the games, connected Okami with [[Toyozawa]] in record time and demonstrated the nation's technological capabilities to the international community.

The games also accelerated the development of the city's hotel industry, international airport capacity, and telecommunications infrastructure. Many of the facilities constructed for the games, including the Okami Metropolitan Gymnasium and the athletes' village (later converted to residential housing), continued to serve the city's residents for decades afterward.

====The 2012 Adonian Games====
Continuing its legacy as a global metropolis, Okami once again stepped onto the world stage when it successfully hosted the 2012 Adonian Games, becoming one of the few cities in Adonia to host the games twice. The 2012 games were notable for their focus on environmental sustainability and technological innovation, showcasing Okami's emergence as a leader in green urban development. The games catalyzed the development of the city's eastern waterfront areas and spurred investment in renewable energy infrastructure and smart city technologies.

===The Quiet Revolution and the Shinkyō Era (1968–2011)===
[[File:Shinkyo Era Okami.png|thumb|right|The Okami Metropolitan Government Building, constructed during the Shinkyō era as a symbol of the Republic's technocratic ambitions.]]
The [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968, which transformed Okaiken from a constitutional monarchy into a Federal Republic, profoundly reshaped Okami's identity and governance. The new republican government, led by Prime Minister [[Yoshimizu Ohno]], viewed the city's very name as an unacceptable relic of the feudal and imperial past. In 1971, Executive Order 99 officially renamed the city {{Nihongo|Shinkyō|新京|'New Capital'}}, severing all linguistic ties to the Honda Shogunate and the Imperial institution.

The Shinkyō era was defined by aggressive modernization and centralization. The newly created Okami Metropolitan Government absorbed the former Okami Prefecture and Okami City, creating a unified administrative entity with unprecedented authority over urban planning, transportation, and economic development. The metropolitan government, dominated by the technocratic [[Republican Party of Okaiken]], pursued a development strategy that prioritized infrastructure, industrial efficiency, and the construction of monumental civic architecture. The Okami Metropolitan Government Building, completed in 1974, was the era's most visible symbol—a massive, brutalist complex that housed the governor's office, the metropolitan assembly, and the centralized urban planning apparatus.

The 1970s and 1980s saw the most dramatic physical transformation of the city since the post-war reconstruction. The Nagashima skyscraper district, zoned for high-rise development in 1968, rapidly filled with office towers that reshaped the skyline. The [[Okami Metro]] system expanded from 4 lines to 10, with the completion of the Asahi Line (1975), the Shitayanagi Line (1978), the Tokuyama Line (1981), and the Namboku Line (1984). The [[Harujima Expressway]] network was extended into a web of elevated highways that threaded between the islands, though the most controversial segment—the proposed waterfront expressway through Minato Ward—was cancelled after sustained public opposition in 1982.

The Shinkyō era also saw the emergence of Okami as a global financial center. The [[Okami Stock Exchange]], reformed and modernized under the Republic's financial deregulation policies, grew rapidly in the 1980s as Okaiken opened its capital markets to foreign investment. The Aoi Financial District, a cluster of glass-and-steel towers built on reclaimed land in the early 1980s, became the physical embodiment of the city's new economic identity. By 1990, the Greater Okami metropolitan area accounted for over 35% of national GDP, a concentration that would only intensify in the following decades.

The late 1980s [[En Crisis|bubble economy]] and its subsequent collapse in the early 1990s had a profound impact on the city. The speculative real estate boom drove land prices in the Aoi district to absurd heights—at the 1989 peak, a square meter of commercial land in Aoi was valued at approximately 円12 million, making it the most expensive real estate on the planet. The collapse of the bubble between 1991 and 1994 devastated the city's construction sector and left a legacy of non-performing loans that took over a decade to resolve. However, the crisis also accelerated the city's pivot toward the service and technology sectors, as the manufacturing and construction industries that had driven the bubble were permanently diminished.

The 2000s saw Okami's emergence as a center of digital innovation. The [[Satori Development Institute]], established in Kanda Ward in 2003, became the nucleus of the city's growing AI and algorithmic governance ecosystem. The city's bid for and successful hosting of the [[2012 Adonian Games]] provided a catalyst for the redevelopment of the eastern waterfront areas and the construction of new transportation infrastructure, including the extension of the Shitayanagi Line to the Tobu waterfront district.

====The Jutsu Restoration and the return of Okami====
The [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011, which restored the constitutional monarchy, brought the city's name back to Okami through a national referendum. The name change was overwhelmingly approved by the city's residents, who had never fully embraced the republican designation despite four decades of official use. The restoration also returned the Imperial family to the city, with the [[Okami Imperial Palace]] once again serving as the primary residence of the Emperor after a 43-year absence.

The restoration era has been characterized by a cultural renaissance that has reinforced Okami's position as the undisputed center of Sanese identity. The [[Idol Liberation Act]] of 2016, which freed idol performers from restrictive contracts, paradoxically accelerated the concentration of the entertainment industry in the capital, as freed artists migrated to the city to establish independent careers. The [[National Iron Body Initiative]], launched in 2015, has further transformed the city's physical and cultural landscape, with the Tatara ward emerging as the epicentre of the {{nihongo|[[physical culture movement]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}.

The [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of 2026, which disrupted the algorithmic systems that manage much of the city's infrastructure, exposed the vulnerabilities of Okami's deep dependence on AI governance. The crisis triggered widespread transit failures, power grid instability, and the temporary collapse of the Satori-managed flood control system, prompting a national debate about the city's over-reliance on automated systems and the need for manual override capabilities.

==Geography==
[[File:Kumagawa Delta Map.svg|thumb|right|500px|The Kumagawa Delta (熊川デルタ) and its 17 islands, including the five historic Honda-era islands (Tenkyō-jima, Tamashima, Nagashima, Mugishima, Ryūbi-jima) and Ikoshima (Okami-Tobu International Airport).]]
[[File:Okami Metropolis in Okaiken.svg|thumb|left|Map of Okami Metropolis within Okaiken]]
===Land Reclamation and Urban Layout===
Okami's distinctive geography was shaped by the monumental "Dragon's Claw Division" project initiated by the Honda clan in the early 17th century. The terraforming of the original marshland delta created five main islands, each with its own distinct character and urban function. This artificial geography, combined with centuries of subsequent land reclamation, has resulted in a city of remarkable geographic complexity.

The original five islands have been extensively modified through land reclamation projects, particularly along the waterfront areas of Ryūbi-jima where the modern industrial zones and port facilities have been expanded. The total area of the metropolis has grown from approximately 1,200 km² in the Honda period to the current 1,985.23 km² through steady reclamation from the sea.

The city's central districts are organized around the Hiryū River, the main waterway that flows through the heart of the metropolis. The river's distinctive turquoise color, which historically attracted the Honda clan to the site, is maintained by the unique mineral composition of the upstream mountain springs and remains one of the city's most distinctive natural features.

====Tenkyō-jima====
[[File:Okami Imperial Palace aerial.png|thumb|right|Aerial view of the Imperial Palace grounds on Tenkyō-jima, with the National Diet visible to the south.]]
{{Nihongo|'''Tenkyō-jima'''|天鏡島|'Heavenly Mirror Island'}}, the northernmost and most heavily fortified of the five islands, serves as the political and imperial heart of the nation. The island covers approximately 18 km² and is bounded by the Kōryū River to the south and the Kashi River to the north. The [[Okami Imperial Palace]] occupies the island's northern quarter, its grounds covering approximately 2.3 km² of meticulously maintained gardens, moats, and historic structures. The southern half of the island houses the [[National Diet Building]], the [[Prime Minister's Official Residence]] (Kantei), the [[Supreme Court of Okaiken]], and the concentrated bureaucratic complex of the [[Kasumigaseki]] district.

The island's street layout remains largely unchanged from the Honda period, with wide boulevards radiating from the palace grounds and narrow side streets that once housed the retainers of the shogunal court. [[File:Okami Sakuradamon Gate.png|thumb|left|The Sakuradamon Gate, the main ceremonial entrance to the Imperial Palace grounds.]]
The {{Nihongo|'''Sakuradamon Gate'''|桜田門|Sakuradamon-mon}}, the main ceremonial entrance to the palace grounds, has been the site of numerous pivotal events in Sanese history, including the [[Sakuradamon Incident]] of 1860 and the [[Imperial Restoration Proclamation]] of 1871. The gate's distinctive black-lacquered timbers and gold fittings, restored in 2012, are among the most photographed landmarks in the nation.

The island's population density is the lowest of the five, as large portions are occupied by government facilities and the palace grounds. The residential areas—concentrated in the island's eastern quarter around the [[Nagatachō]] district—are among the most expensive in the nation, with property values driven by proximity to the Imperial Palace and the political district. The [[Nagatachō]] area, which houses the metropolitan government offices and the residences of senior civil servants, is characterized by low-rise, high-security apartment buildings and a conspicuous absence of the commercial activity that defines the other islands.

[[File:Okami Kasumigaseki district.png|thumb|right|The Kasumigaseki bureaucratic complex, with the Ministry of Finance building at center.]]
The [[Kasumigaseki]] bureaucratic complex, stretching along the island's southern ridge, employs approximately 45,000 civil servants in a dense cluster of ministry buildings that ranges from the neoclassical [[Ministry of Finance (Okaiken)|Ministry of Finance]] building (1936) to the modernist [[Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry (Okaiken)|METI]] headquarters (1986). The complex's architecture reflects the shifting priorities of the Sanese state: the pre-war buildings convey imperial authority through monumental stone facades and bronze ornamentation, while the post-war structures project technocratic efficiency through glass curtain walls and exposed concrete. The {{Nihongo|''Kasumigaseki Hill''|霞が関の丘|Kasumigaseki no oka}} complex, which includes the [[National Police Agency (Okaiken)|National Police Agency]] and the [[Public Prosecutors Office]], is the nerve center of the Sanese law enforcement establishment.

====Mugishima====
{{Nihongo|'''Mugishima'''|麦島|'Barley Island'}}, split by the Garyū and Sōryū rivers, is the traditional commercial heart of the city. The island covers approximately 22 km² and is home to the city's most prestigious shopping districts, including the [[Miyoshi-suji]] luxury corridor and the historic [[Nakamichi]] merchant quarter. The island's eastern waterfront, known as the {{Nihongo|''Ryōgoku-higashi''|両国東|'East of Both Provinces'}}, houses the city's premier fish and produce markets. The western interior of the island contains the densest concentration of the city's ''washi'' paper workshops, a tradition that dates to the Honda period and gave the city its modern name. The island's population of approximately 380,000 makes it the second most densely populated of the five.

[[File:Miyoshi-suji.png|thumb|right|The Miyoshi-suji luxury corridor along the Garyū River waterfront.]]
The [[Miyoshi-suji]], the island's most famous thoroughfare, is a 1.2-kilometer boulevard that runs along the Garyū River waterfront from the [[Kōryū Bridge]] in the north to the [[Sōryū Bridge]] in the south. The street's origins as the primary merchant corridor of the Honda period are visible in its surviving historic storefronts—narrow, deep-lot buildings with traditional latticed facades that now house luxury boutiques and high-end restaurants. The Miyoshi-suji's transformation from a wholesale mercantile district to an international luxury shopping destination was completed in the 1980s, when the [[Miyoshi-suji Redevelopment Agency]] oversaw the renovation of the historic facades and the construction of the modern department stores that anchor the street's southern end.

[[File:Okami washi paper workshop.png|thumb|left|A traditional washi paper workshop in the Kami-no-chō district.]]
The island's ''washi'' paper district, centered on the {{Nihongo|''Kami-no-chō''|紙の町|'Paper Town'}} neighborhood in the western interior, is one of the few surviving traditional craft districts in the central wards. The district's paper workshops, many operated by the same families for over ten generations, produce the high-quality ''washi'' used in the Imperial Palace's official documents, the National Diet's legislative records, and the fine-art prints that are among Okami's most prestigious cultural exports. The [[Okami Paper Museum]], housed in a restored 18th-century paper warehouse, documents the craft's history and offers workshops in traditional papermaking techniques.

====Nagashima====
[[File:Okami Nagashima.png|thumb|right|The Nagashima Tower District skyline, viewed from the Hiryū River.]]
{{Nihongo|'''Nagashima'''|長島|'Long Island'}}, the largest of the five at approximately 35 km², is the city's modern commercial and financial center. The island's flat terrain and central location made it the natural site for the skyscraper district that has come to define the Okami skyline. The [[Nagashima Tower District]], a cluster of over 60 high-rise office buildings, is home to the [[Okami Stock Exchange]], the headquarters of the nation's largest banks and insurance companies, and the regional offices of numerous multinational corporations.

[[File:Okami sora-no-michi walkway.png|thumb|left|The sora-no-michi elevated walkway network connecting Nagashima's office towers.]]
The Tower District's development was shaped by the 1968 metropolitan zoning plan, which designated Nagashima for high-rise construction and established the {{Nihongo|''sora-no-michi''|空の道|'sky path'}}—a network of elevated pedestrian walkways connecting the major office towers at the second-floor level. The ''sora-no-michi'', designed to separate pedestrian traffic from vehicular traffic and to provide climate-controlled circulation during the humid summer months, has become one of the city's most distinctive urban features. The network now extends over 8 kilometers and connects 47 buildings, with restaurants, shops, and garden terraces positioned along the routes.

The island's southern quarter retains elements of its historical character as the residential district of the samurai class, with several preserved ''bukeyashiki'' estates now functioning as museums. [[File:Okami Honda Estate Museum.png|thumb|right|The Honda Estate Museum, a preserved bukeyashiki estate in southern Nagashima.]]
The [[Honda Estate Museum]], the largest and best-preserved of the surviving samurai residences, occupies a 2-hectare site with original Honda-period gardens, a tea house, and a collection of armor, weapons, and household artifacts from the Honda Shogunate. The museum's {{Nihongo|''shoin-zukuri''|書院造}} reception hall, with its gold-leaf sliding doors and tatami-matted audience chambers, is considered one of the finest surviving examples of shogunal residential architecture.

The island's population of approximately 520,000 includes a high proportion of corporate expatriates and single professionals. The residential areas are concentrated in the island's eastern and western quarters, where high-rise apartment towers provide the dense housing stock required by the island's workforce. The [[Shinbashi]] district, at the island's southeastern corner, has evolved into one of the world's densest and most economically powerful urban areas, with a daytime population density exceeding 50,000 per km².

====Tamashima====
[[File:Okami Neon District night.png|thumb|right|The Neon District at night, with the characteristic glow of hundreds of neon signs.]]
{{Nihongo|'''Tamashima'''|玉島|'Jewel Island'}}, separated from the northern islands by the wide Kōryū River, is the entertainment and cultural soul of the city. The island covers approximately 28 km² and is home to the world-famous {{Nihongo|'''Neon District'''|ネオン区|Neon-ku}}, a dense labyrinth of bars, nightclubs, live music venues, pachinko parlors, and restaurants that operates at full intensity from dusk until dawn. The Neon District's origins as the chaotic labor encampment of the Honda period are still visible in its irregular street grid and narrow alleyways, which defy the orthogonal planning of the other islands.

[[File:Okami Kabuki-za Theater.png|thumb|right|The Kabuki-za Theater in the Ginza district, home to traditional kabuki performances since 1889.]]
The island is also home to the [[Kabuki-za Theater]], the [[Okami Metropolitan Opera House]], and the historic {{Nihongo|''Yoshiwara''|吉原}} entertainment quarter, which has operated continuously since 1617. The [[Okami Idol Festival]], the largest music event in Fosia, is held annually in the Tamashima district, drawing over 500,000 attendees to open-air stages and venue showcases across the island.

The island's western quarter, known as {{Nihongo|'''Tama'''|多摩}}, underwent a dramatic transformation in the post-war period that distinguishes it sharply from the chaotic Neon District to the east. While the eastern half of Tamashima retained its organic, Honda-period street grid and its identity as the city's entertainment zone, the western Tama district was comprehensively redeveloped under the 1968 metropolitan plan as a modern commercial center. It was here, on the flat, cleared land of the former Tama military barracks, that the [[Sanese Metabolism]] architectural movement found its most ambitious expression.

[[File:Okami Tama Center.png|thumb|left|The Okami Tama Center, the most iconic built work of the Sanese Metabolism movement.]]
The [[Okami Tama Center]], designed by [[Kishō Kurokawa]] and completed in 1972, is the defining landmark of the Tama district and the most iconic built work of the Metabolism movement. The Center's striking inverted pyramid structure—a massive concrete frame from which 144 modular "capsule" units are suspended—was conceived as a demonstration of the Metabolist principle of replaceable parts: individual capsules could be removed and replaced as technology and needs evolved, while the core structure remained permanent. The Center originally contained a mix of residential and office capsules, each a self-contained 2.5m × 4m unit with integrated furniture, communications systems, and climate control. The paradox of the Okami Tama Center—and of Metabolism in general—is that the buildings designed to be replaceable became permanent. By the 1990s, the capsules had deteriorated significantly, and the original vision of regular replacement had proven economically and logistically impractical. The Center was extensively renovated between 1998 and 2002, with the original capsules replaced by modernized units that maintain the exterior aesthetic while upgrading the interior systems. The renovated Center now houses a mix of residential units, creative industry offices, and the [[Metabolism Archive Museum]], which documents the movement's history and influence.

The Tama district's broader urban fabric reflects the Metabolist influence. The [[Tama Central Park]], the 96-hectare green space that serves the western suburbs, was designed according to Metabolist principles as a {{Nihongo|"living landscape"|生景|Ikkei}} whose plantings and water features would evolve over time in response to the surrounding community's needs. The park's distinctive terraced gardens, which cascade down the natural slope of the Tama Heights toward the river, were designed to be reconfigurable—the original plans called for the terraces to be periodically reshaped by community input, though in practice the layout has remained largely fixed since the 1980s. The [[Tama Island Transit Hub]], completed in 1978, is another significant Metabolist work, with its distinctive elevated platform design where the Metro station, bus terminal, and commercial concourse are stacked in a hierarchical arrangement directly inspired by the concept of {{Nihongo|"artificial land"|人工地|Jinkōchi}}.

The island's population of approximately 440,000 includes a large proportion of service industry workers, artists, and musicians. The stark contrast between the organic Neon District and the planned Tama district—separated by the [[Miyoshi-suji]] boulevard that runs across the island's midline—has made Tamashima a study in urban dualism, where the city's oldest and newest identities exist side by side.

====Ryūbi-jima====
[[File:Okami Tatara shipyards.png|thumb|right|The Tatara shipyards on Ryūbi-jima, with the container port visible to the east.]]
{{Nihongo|'''Ryūbi-jima'''|竜尾島|'Dragon Tail Island'}}, the southernmost island, is the industrial and maritime gateway of the city. The island covers approximately 30 km² and is dominated by the [[Okami Industrial Zone]] and the heavy shipyards of [[Tatara Ward]]. The island's eastern docks have been the primary gateway for continental trade for over four centuries, and the modern container port facilities handle approximately 40% of the nation's maritime cargo by value.

The island's western portion has undergone significant redevelopment since the 2000s, with former industrial sites converted to residential and commercial use. The {{Nihongo|''Tatara Circuit''|多々羅回廊|Tatara-kairō}}, a cluster of premium gyms and fitness facilities that has become the epicentre of the {{nihongo|[[physical culture movement]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}, occupies a 2 km stretch of the island's western waterfront. The [[Tatara Fitness Waterfront]], a 3.2-kilometer athletic park featuring a 10-lane swimming complex, a beach volleyball arena, and the 30-meter {{Nihongo|''Tatara Tower''|多々羅塔|Tatara-tō}} climbing wall, has become one of the island's most recognizable landmarks.

The island's industrial heritage is preserved in the [[Tatara Shipyard Museum]], which documents the island's 400-year history of shipbuilding and maritime trade. The museum's centerpiece is the {{Nihongo|''Kairyū-maru''|海龍丸}}, a fully restored Honda-period trading vessel that was recovered from the Kairyū River in 1987. The island's eastern container port, operated by the [[Okami Port Authority]], is one of the most automated cargo facilities in Adonia, with Satori-integrated crane systems that can load and unload container ships with minimal human intervention.

The island's population of approximately 610,000 is the most economically diverse of the five, ranging from dockworkers and industrial laborers to fitness entrepreneurs and tech workers. [[File:Okami Kwangju-cho district.png|thumb|left|The Kwangju-chō district on Ryūbi-jima, with its distinctive geometric tilework and pitched roofs.]]
The [[Kwangju-chō]] district, the cultural and commercial center of the city's [[Kwangju people|Kwangju]] community, is located in the island's northwestern quarter, where the distinctive geometric tilework and pitched roofs of Kwangju traditional architecture stand in sharp contrast to the surrounding industrial landscape.


===Rivers and Waterways===
[[File:Okami Hiryu River turquoise.png|thumb|right|The turquoise waters of the Hiryū River, the city's most distinctive natural feature, flowing through the central wards.]]
The four arterial canals created by the Dragon's Claw Division—the {{Nihongo|Kōryū|荒龍|'Wild Dragon'}}, {{Nihongo|Garyū|我龍|'Our Dragon'}}, {{Nihongo|Sōryū|蒼龍|'Blue Dragon'}}, and {{Nihongo|Kairyū|海龍|'Sea Dragon'}}—remain the primary waterways of the city and serve crucial functions in transportation, drainage, and urban identity. The canals range from 80 to 200 metres in width and are spanned by over 120 bridges, ranging from the historic stone arches of the Honda period to the modern cable-stayed spans connecting the skyscraper districts.

The {{Nihongo|'''Hiryū River'''|飛龍川|'Flying Dragon River'}}, the main waterway that flows through the heart of the metropolis, is the most visually distinctive feature of the city's geography. The river's characteristic turquoise color—caused by the unique mineral composition of the upstream mountain springs—was noted by the Honda clan's surveyors and remains one of the city's most iconic natural features. The river's banks are lined with pedestrian promenades, cherry trees, and waterfront parks that serve as the primary recreational space for the central wards.

The original Kumagawa and Kashi rivers, whose delta was the site of the original settlement, continue to flow along the northern and western boundaries of the city. The Kumagawa River, partially dammed and diverted during the Dragon's Claw project, still carries approximately 40% of its original flow and serves as the primary source of the city's freshwater supply. The Kashi River, which forms the northern boundary of Tenkyō-jima, is navigable by small craft and is used extensively for recreational boating.

===Microclimates===
[[File:Okami urban heat island thermal.png|thumb|left|Thermal imaging of the Okami urban heat island effect, showing the temperature differential between the central wards and the western suburbs.]]
The city's complex geography—the five islands, the river network, the coastal position, and the dense urban fabric—creates several distinct microclimates across the metropolitan area.

The five islands experience a pronounced urban heat island effect, with temperatures in the central wards regularly exceeding those of the western suburbs by 2–3 °C. This effect is most pronounced at night and during the summer months, when the dense concrete and asphalt of the urban core retains heat long after sunset. The {{Nihongo|''neba-ru''|ねばる|'persisting'}} humidity, as residents term the oppressive summer nights, is a defining feature of the Okami summer experience and drives the city's enormous demand for air conditioning, which in turn strains the electrical grid during peak periods.

The river corridors create localized cooling effects, particularly along the Hiryū and Kōryū rivers, where daytime temperatures can be 1–2 °C lower than the surrounding urban areas. The waterfront promenades along these rivers serve as natural gathering points during the summer, and the city has invested heavily in riverside green spaces and misting systems to amplify the cooling effect.

The western suburbs, which extend into the foothills of the Okami mountain range, experience significantly cooler temperatures and higher rainfall than the central wards. The {{Nihongo|Tama Heights|多摩丘陵|Tama-kyūryō}} area, at elevations of 100–200 metres, receives approximately 15% more annual precipitation than the city centre and is more prone to fog and low cloud cover during the winter months. The Tama area's cooler microclimate has made it a desirable residential location for families seeking relief from the urban heat island, and the area's population has grown steadily despite its distance from the commercial core.

The coastal areas of Minato and Tatara wards, exposed to the Shendan Ocean, experience stronger onshore breezes and more moderate temperatures than the inland wards. The {{Nihongo|''shiokaze''|潮風|'tide wind'}}—the salt-laden ocean breeze that sweeps through the eastern wards in the afternoon—is a welcome relief during the summer but also accelerates the corrosion of building facades and infrastructure, requiring the city to invest heavily in marine-grade construction materials for coastal developments.

===Parks and Green Spaces===
[[File:Okami Chuo Park.png|thumb|right|Okami Chūō Park on Mugishima, with the cherry blossom avenue along the Hiryū River.]]
Despite its reputation as a hyper-dense urban environment, Okami maintains an extensive network of parks, gardens, and green spaces that cover approximately 12% of the metropolitan area. The city's park system is managed by the Okami Metropolitan Government and is organized into four categories: national parks, metropolitan parks, ward parks, and riverside green corridors.

The [[Okami Chūō Park]], located on Mugishima along the banks of the Hiryū River, is the city's most visited green space. The park's 45 hectares include the historic [[Kofukuji Temple]] gardens, a large ornamental lake, and a renowned cherry blossom avenue that draws over 2 million visitors during the annual [[Cherry Blossom Festival]]. The [[Imperial Palace East Gardens]], open to the public year-round, provide 20 hectares of meticulously maintained traditional gardens on Tenkyō-jima.

The [[Tama Central Park]], at 96 hectares the largest park in the metropolitan area, serves the western suburbs and includes extensive sports facilities, a botanical garden, and the [[Okami Metropolitan Zoo]]. The [[Ueno Park]] complex on the northern edge of the special wards houses the [[Okami National Museum]], the [[National Museum of Modern Art]], and the [[Okami Metropolitan Art Museum]], as well as the city's oldest and most famous cherry blossom grove.

The riverside green corridors—pedestrian and cycling paths that run along the banks of the four arterial canals and the Hiryū River—provide over 60 km of continuous green space through the heart of the city. These corridors, developed primarily since the 1990s, have become essential recreational infrastructure for the dense central wards and are heavily used by joggers, cyclists, and commuters.


=== Climate ===
Okami is located in the [[wikipedia:humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] zone ([[wikipedia:Köppen climate classification|Köppen]] ''Cfa''), with long, hot and humid summers and mild winters. Its winters are generally very mild, with July being the coldest month having an average high of 19.5&nbsp;°C. The city does not see snowfall. Spring in Okami starts off mild, but ends up being hot and humid. It also tends to be Okami's wettest season, with the {{Nihongo|''[[East Fosian rainy season|tsuyu]]''|梅雨|tsuyu|"plum rain"}}—the rainy season—occurring between early May to late June. Summers are very hot and humid. In the months of July and August, the average daily high temperature approaches 32&nbsp;°C, while average nighttime temperatures typically hover around 27&nbsp;°C. Fall in Okami sees a cooling trend, with the early part of the season resembling summer while the latter part of fall remains quite warm and mild.

