==Economy==
{{Main|Economy of Okami}}
Okami is the economic powerhouse of Okaiken and one of the major financial centers of Adonia. The Greater Okami metropolitan area accounts for approximately one-third of the nation's GDP and serves as the headquarters for the majority of Okaiken's largest corporations, financial institutions, and international organizations. The city's economic dominance has intensified in the 21st century, as the concentration of financial services, technology, media, and entertainment industries in the capital has accelerated even as many of the nation's rural and regional prefectures experience population decline and economic contraction.

===The Okami magnet and regional depopulation===
The most significant structural trend in the modern Sanese economy is the accelerating concentration of population, capital, and talent in the Okami metropolitan area. While the nation's total population has been declining since 2015—driven by a total fertility rate of 1.26 and an aging demographic—the Greater Okami area has continued to grow, absorbing young workers from every region of the archipelago. Between 2015 and 2025, the Okami metropolitan area gained approximately 1.2 million residents through internal migration, while the combined population of the remaining prefectures declined by over 2.8 million.

The {{nihongo|"Okami magnet"|大紙磁石|Okami-jishaku}}, as economists have termed the phenomenon, is driven by several reinforcing factors. The city's financial sector and its technology industry offer salaries 40–60% higher than the national average, and the concentration of corporate headquarters in the Aoi and Chuo wards creates a self-reinforcing cycle of opportunity and investment. The [[Satori AI]] ecosystem, centred on the [[Satori Development Institute]] in Kanda, has attracted the nation's most talented software engineers and data scientists, while the [[Okami Stock Exchange]]'s dominance of capital markets ensures that the city remains the indispensable gateway for both domestic and foreign investment.

The cultural dimension of the Okami magnet is equally important. The city's position as the undisputed centre of Sanese popular culture—music, fashion, gaming, and entertainment—has made it the only viable location for ambitious young people seeking careers in the creative industries. The [[PYRAMID30]] music charts, the nation's most influential pop culture barometer, are dominated by Okami-based artists and labels, and the city's fashion districts—particularly the [[Tamashima]] Neon District and the [[Aoi]] luxury corridor—set trends that are followed throughout the archipelago. For young Sanese, the choice is increasingly framed as "Okami or nothing": the regional cities offer stability and tradition, but the capital offers the possibility of a career in the industries that define modern Sanese identity.

The government has attempted to counter the Okami magnet through various regional revitalisation programmes, including the [[Regional Innovation Cities]] initiative and tax incentives for companies that relocate headquarters to regional prefectures. These programmes have had limited success, as the economic logic of agglomeration—the benefits that firms and workers derive from proximity to one another—overwhelms the modest financial incentives offered by the state. The [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]'s 2026 "Digital Decentralisation" proposal, which would mandate remote work for 30% of government employees, is the most ambitious attempt to date, but its implementation remains uncertain in the wake of the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]].

===Financial Services===
[[File:Okami Stock Exchange.png|thumb|right|The Okami Stock Exchange in the Aoi Financial District.]]
The city's financial district, centered on the Aoi and Chuo wards, is home to the [[Okami Stock Exchange]], one of the largest stock exchanges in Adonia by market capitalization. The exchange lists over 3,500 companies and plays a crucial role in the nation's capital markets. Major financial institutions headquartered in Okami include the [[Bank of Okaiken]], the [[Sanese Development Bank]], and the nation's largest commercial banks and insurance companies.

The Okami International Financial Center, located in the Minato waterfront development zone, has emerged as a major hub for international finance, attracting regional headquarters of multinational corporations and serving as a gateway for foreign investment into Okaiken.

===Technology and Innovation===
Okami has established itself as a leading center for technology and innovation. [[File:Okami Satori Development Institute.png|thumb|left|The Satori Development Institute in Kanda Ward, the nucleus of the city's AI ecosystem.]]
The city is home to the [[Satori Development Institute]], the nation's primary research facility for artificial intelligence and algorithmic governance. The Kanda district houses numerous technology startups and research laboratories, while the Shinagawa ward hosts major electronics manufacturers and telecommunications companies.

The city's commitment to smart city development has made it a global leader in urban technology. The integration of the Satori algorithmic system into city infrastructure has enabled the development of autonomous transportation networks, smart grid energy systems, and AI-powered public services.

===Pop culture and entertainment===
Okami is the undisputed centre of Sanese popular culture, and the entertainment industry is one of the city's most important economic sectors and a primary driver of the Okami magnet effect. The city's music, fashion, gaming, and media industries generate an estimated 円1.24 trillion in annual revenue and employ approximately 420,000 workers in the metropolitan area.

[[File:Okami O-Pop Neon District.png|thumb|right|An O-Pop performance in the Tamashima Neon District.]]
The [[O-Pop]] industry, centred on the [[Tamashima]] entertainment district and the [[Aoi]] label corridor, is the city's most visible cultural export. [[File:Okami PYRAMID30 studio.png|thumb|right|A recording studio in the Sound District, the 3-kilometre corridor that houses the O-Pop production infrastructure.]]
The [[PYRAMID30]] charts, compiled weekly from sales data, streaming figures, and radio airplay, are the definitive measure of commercial success in Sanese popular music, and an appearance on the PYRAMID30 is considered the essential credential for any artist seeking national recognition. The major O-Pop labels—including [[Siren Records]], [[Tensei Music]], and [[Crystal Wave Entertainment]]—are all headquartered in the Aoi-Chuo corridor, and the industry's production infrastructure (recording studios, talent agencies, choreography studios, and marketing firms) is concentrated within a 3-kilometre radius that has been termed the {{nihongo|"Sound District"|音域|On-iki}}.

The [[Idol Liberation Act]] of 2016, which ended the restrictive contract system that had governed the idol industry for decades, paradoxically accelerated the concentration of idol culture in Okami. The freed idols, no longer bound to their original agencies, migrated to the capital to establish independent careers, and the resulting proliferation of small labels and independent production companies made Okami the only city with the critical mass of talent, infrastructure, and media access needed to sustain a modern idol career. The [[Okami Idol Festival]], held annually in the Tamashima district, draws over 500,000 attendees and is one of the largest music events in Fosia.

The gaming and digital content sector is equally concentrated. The [[Okami Esports Arena]], a 30,000-seat venue opened in 2023, hosts the annual [[Shendan Cup]] tournament and has established the city as the premier competitive gaming destination in the East Fosian sphere. The virtual idol industry—AI-generated performers who exist only as digital avatars—has become a uniquely Sanese cultural export, with the most successful virtual idol, {{nihongo|''Satori-hime''|悟り姫|Awakening Princess}}, generating over 円8 billion in annual merchandise and streaming revenue.

===The National Iron Body Initiative and the fitness economy===
{{Main|Cult of Iron|National Iron Body Initiative}}
Okami is the administrative centre and the primary beneficiary of the {{nihongo|'''[[National Iron Body Initiative]]'''|国家鉄身計画|Kokka Tesshin Keikaku}}, the government's flagship fitness and public health programme launched by [[Crown Prince Tomohito]] in 2015. The Initiative, which frames physical fitness as a matter of national security and civic virtue, has its headquarters in the [[Tatara Sports Complex]] and its policy coordination office in the [[Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (Okaiken)|Ministry of Health]] building in Chuo Ward.

The Initiative has had a transformative effect on the Okami economy. The {{nihongo|'''fitness industrial complex'''|健産業|Ken-sangyō}}—the commercial ecosystem that has grown around the {{nihongo|[[physical culture movement]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}—is centred on Okami, where the concentration of fitness equipment manufacturers, supplement companies, athletic apparel brands, and recovery wellness facilities generates an estimated 円420 billion in annual revenue. [[File:Okami Tatara Circuit gym.png|thumb|right|A premium gym on the Tatara Circuit, the epicentre of the physical culture movement.]]
The [[Tatara]] ward, historically the city's heavy industrial zone, has been partially redeveloped as the {{nihongo|"Fitness District"|健区|Ken-ku}}, a cluster of premium gyms, supplement research laboratories, and athletic recovery centres that caters to the city's estimated 1.8 million regular gym-goers.

The Initiative's subsidies for athletic recovery—including the {{nihongo|''Tesshin-yu''|鉄身湯|Iron Body Bath}} onsen programme, which provides subsidised access to thermal recovery facilities for registered athletes—have created a secondary market in athletic tourism, with Okami's onsen hotels and recovery spas catering to fitness tourists from across the archipelago. The [[Okami Marathon]], held each February, attracts over 40,000 runners and generates an estimated 円28 billion in tourism revenue, while [[File:Okami Mr Adonia championship.png|thumb|left|The Mr. Adonia bodybuilding championship at the Tatara Sports Complex.]]
the [[Mr. Adonia]] bodybuilding championship, held at the Tatara Sports Complex, draws competitors and spectators from across the East Fosian sphere.

The physical culture movement's cultural influence is particularly visible in the Tatara ward, where the {{nihongo|"guardian physique"|守護体格|Shugo-taikaku}}—the muscular, athletic body type promoted by the Iron Body ideology—has become the dominant aesthetic for young men. The ward's gyms, supplement shops, and protein restaurants form a distinctive commercial ecosystem that has been termed the {{nihongo|"Tatara Circuit"|多々羅回廊|Tatara-kairō}}, and the area has become a pilgrimage destination for fitness enthusiasts from across Okaiken.

====The Expansion of Fitness Culture (2020–2026)====
Since 2020, the fitness culture that originated in the Tatara ward has expanded dramatically across the metropolitan area, driven by a convergence of government policy, corporate investment, and cultural normalization. The [[National Iron Body Initiative]]'s 2020 amendment, which extended subsidized gym memberships to all metropolitan residents aged 16–65, triggered a surge in gym construction across the special wards. Between 2020 and 2025, the number of commercial fitness facilities in the metropolitan area grew from approximately 2,800 to over 5,200, with the most dramatic growth occurring in the central wards—Aoi, Chuo, and Chiyoda—where premium "boutique fitness" studios targeting the professional class have proliferated.

[[File:Okami Aoi Fitness Corridor.png|thumb|right|A boutique fitness studio in the Aoi Fitness Corridor, catering to the city's professional class.]]
The {{Nihongo|'''White-Collar Iron'''|白書鉄|Hakusho-tetsu}} phenomenon, as the media has termed the adoption of fitness culture by the office worker class, represents a significant cultural shift. The Tatara Circuit's working-class aesthetic—raw iron, chalk, and sweat—has been adapted for the corporate market through a new generation of "luxury fitness" establishments that combine high-end equipment, designer interiors, and the Satori-integrated workout tracking systems. The [[Aoi Fitness Corridor]], a stretch of premium gyms and recovery spas along the Aoi-Chuo border, has become the preferred destination for the city's bankers, lawyers, and technology executives, who treat the {{Nihongo|''tesshin-sagyō''|鉄身作業|'iron body work'}}—the daily workout—as a non-negotiable element of professional life.

The corporate sector has embraced fitness culture as both a productivity strategy and a status marker. Over 60% of the companies listed on the [[Okami Stock Exchange]] now offer on-site gym facilities or subsidized fitness memberships as employee benefits, and the [[Okami Metropolitan Government]] has mandated that all new commercial buildings over 10,000 m² include fitness infrastructure. The [[Satori Development Institute]]'s research division has published extensively on the correlation between physical fitness and cognitive performance, providing the scientific justification for what critics have termed the "mandatory wellness" regime.

The expansion of fitness culture has not been without controversy. The [[DSP]] opposition has criticized the National Iron Body Initiative as a form of state coercion over individual lifestyle choices, and the [[Sanese Communist Party]] has argued that the subsidized gym membership program disproportionately benefits the affluent wards where gym facilities are concentrated. The {{Nihongo|''Ken-sangyō Seisaku Renmei''|健産業政策連盟|Fitness Industry Policy Federation}}, the lobbying arm of the fitness industrial complex, has become one of the most powerful interest groups in the metropolitan assembly, advocating for expanded subsidies and mandatory fitness infrastructure requirements.

====Landscape Architecture and the Fitness City====
The most visible physical manifestation of the fitness culture's expansion is the transformation of Okami's public landscape architecture. Since 2020, the Okami Metropolitan Government has invested approximately 円180 billion in the {{Nihongo|'''Fitness City Initiative'''|健都市計画|Ken-toshi keikaku}}, a comprehensive redesign of the city's parks, promenades, and public spaces to accommodate and encourage physical activity.

The Initiative's design philosophy, developed by the [[Okami Metropolitan Urban Design Institute]] in collaboration with the [[Satori Development Institute]], treats the entire city as a potential fitness environment. The traditional distinction between "recreational" parks and "athletic" facilities has been dissolved in favor of a {{Nihongo|''taishō-teki kūkan''|対称的空間|'adaptive space'}} approach, where public spaces are designed to serve multiple functions—recreation, transportation, and exercise—simultaneously.

[[File:Okami Green Ring running path.png|thumb|right|The Green Ring running path following the Okami Loop Line route.]]
The most significant element of the Fitness City Initiative is the {{Nihongo|'''Green Ring'''|緑輪|Ryokurin}}, a 42-kilometer continuous circuit of running paths, cycling lanes, and calisthenics stations that follows the route of the [[Okami Loop Line]] railway. The Green Ring, which opened in stages between 2022 and 2025, connects all 20 special wards and provides a standardized fitness infrastructure that can be used by runners, cyclists, and walkers regardless of their starting point. The Ring's design incorporates the Satori system's real-time crowd monitoring, with LED-embedded path markers that change color to indicate congestion levels and guide users toward less crowded segments.

[[File:Okami Hiryu Promenade fitness.png|thumb|left|The redesigned Hiryū River Promenade, with its three-lane system and outdoor fitness stations.]]
The [[Hiryū River Promenade]], the city's most popular riverside green corridor, has been extensively redesigned under the Initiative. The original 3-meter pedestrian path has been expanded to 12 meters and divided into three lanes: a high-speed running lane, a cycling lane, and a walking lane. The promenade now features 28 outdoor fitness stations—pull-up bars, parallel bars, stretching platforms, and resistance training equipment—positioned at 1.5-kilometer intervals along the river. The stations, designed by the [[Okami Metropolitan Urban Design Institute]] with input from the [[National Iron Body Initiative]]'s exercise science division, are constructed from marine-grade stainless steel and feature integrated Satori sensors that count repetitions and provide form guidance through embedded display screens.

[[File:Okami Chuo Park iron garden.png|thumb|right|The 'iron garden' at Okami Chūō Park, where strength training equipment is integrated into the traditional landscape.]]
The redesign of [[Okami Chūō Park]] on Mugishima demonstrates the Initiative's most ambitious integration of landscape architecture and fitness culture. The park's traditional ornamental gardens have been preserved, but the surrounding open spaces have been transformed into a {{Nihongo|''kinō-teki enchi''|機能的園地|'functional garden'}}—a landscape designed to facilitate exercise while maintaining aesthetic quality. The park's new features include a 2.4-kilometer rubberized running loop that winds through the cherry blossom groves, a 400-meter athletics-standard track surrounding the ornamental lake, and an outdoor "iron garden" with weather-resistant strength training equipment positioned among the traditional plantings. The design has been controversial—traditionalists have criticized the intrusion of industrial fitness equipment into the park's historic landscape—but the Metropolitan Government has defended it as a necessary adaptation of public space to the city's evolving needs.

The Tatara ward's landscape has been the most dramatically transformed. [[File:Okami Tatara Fitness Waterfront.png|thumb|left|The Tatara Fitness Waterfront, with the Tatara Tower climbing wall visible at center.]]
The {{Nihongo|'''Tatara Fitness Waterfront'''|多々羅健水際|Tatara-ken-mizugiwa}}, a 3.2-kilometer stretch of the Ryūbi-jima western shoreline, has been converted from derelict industrial land into a state-of-the-art athletic park. The waterfront features a 10-lane swimming complex, a beach volleyball arena, a 5-kilometer coastal running path, and the {{Nihongo|''Tatara Tower''|多々羅塔|Tatara-tō}}—a 30-meter climbing wall that has become one of the ward's most recognizable landmarks. The waterfront's design incorporates the {{Nihongo|''Tesshin-yu''|鉄身湯|Iron Body Bath}} thermal recovery facility, where athletes can transition directly from training to onsen recovery without leaving the complex.


===Industry===
While the city's economy has shifted toward services, Okami retains significant industrial capacity. The Tatara ward houses the [[Okami Industrial Zone]], one of the nation's largest concentrations of heavy industry. The zone includes major shipyards, steel mills, and chemical processing facilities. The historic shipyards of Tatara have produced vessels for the Sanese navy and merchant marine for centuries.

The light manufacturing sector is concentrated in the Kita and Uonuma wards, producing electronics, precision instruments, and consumer goods. The city's printing and publishing industry, centered on the traditional paper-making districts, remains significant and reflects the city's historical identity as "Big Paper."

===Tourism and Hospitality===
Okami attracts millions of domestic and international visitors annually, making tourism a significant sector of the economy. The city's cultural attractions, including historic temples, museums, and entertainment districts, draw visitors from across Adonia. The hotel and hospitality industry is concentrated in the central wards, with luxury accommodations in Chiyoda, Aoi, and Tama and business hotels throughout the commercial districts.

===The Okami Metropolitan Region and the Northern Corridor===
[[File:Okami Northern Corridor rail.png|thumb|right|The Tokuyama-Okami Express Railway, the primary transit link between the capital and the Northern Corridor.]]
The Greater Okami metropolitan area does not exist in isolation; it is the southern terminus and economic engine of the {{Nihongo|'''Northern Harujima Corridor'''|北春島回廊|Kita-Harujima kairō}}, the chain of industrial and agricultural prefectures stretching northward along the western coast of Harujima. The relationship between the capital and its neighboring prefectures—[[Yamagata Prefecture|Yamagata]], [[Akita Prefecture|Akita]], [[Yonezawa Prefecture|Yonezawa]], [[Fukushima Prefecture|Fukushima]], and [[Tokuyama Prefecture|Tokuyama]]—is one of profound economic interdependence and growing demographic imbalance.

