The Northern Corridor prefectures supply the capital with essential resources and labor. The agricultural output of Yamagata and Fukushima—particularly rice, fruit, and dairy products—feeds the metropolitan area's 36.9 million residents, with an estimated 40% of the food consumed in Okami originating in the Northern Corridor. The heavy industrial output of Akita and Tokuyama—steel, petrochemicals, and shipbuilding components—feeds the capital's construction sector and the Tatara Industrial Zone. The precision manufacturing of Yonezawa, particularly its textile and optical equipment industries, supplies the fashion and technology sectors of the central wards.

The flow of labor from the Northern Corridor to the capital is the most consequential demographic trend in the relationship. Between 2010 and 2020, the five Northern Corridor prefectures lost a combined 420,000 residents to net out-migration, with the majority of those departing being young workers aged 20–35. The [[Okami magnet]] effect draws these workers with the promise of higher salaries, career opportunities, and the cultural vibrancy that the regional cities cannot match. The result is a vicious cycle: as the young depart, the regional economies contract, the tax base erodes, and the remaining population ages, further reducing the economic vitality of the prefectures and driving even more young people to seek opportunity in the capital.

The [[Tokuyama]]-Okami relationship is the most intense of the Northern Corridor connections. At only 120 km from the capital, Tokuyama functions as Okami's primary industrial satellite. The [[Tokuyama Heavy Industries]] complex, one of the nation's largest shipbuilding and steel facilities, produces components for the Tatara shipyards and the capital's construction sector. The [[Tokuyama-Okami Express Railway]], completed in 1989, provides a 45-minute connection that has effectively made Tokuyama a commuter suburb for the capital's industrial workforce. The two cities' economies are so deeply integrated that the [[Okaiken Times]] has described Tokuyama as "the workshop of Okami, separated by a mountain range and a political boundary."

The [[Fukushima]]-Okami relationship is more complex. Fukushima's nuclear energy facilities, which generate approximately 15% of the nation's electricity, are critical to powering the capital's enormous energy demand. The [[Fukushima-Okami Power Corridor]], a network of high-voltage transmission lines completed in the 1970s, transmits electricity directly from the Fukushima plants to the metropolitan grid. This energy dependency has created political tensions, as Fukushima residents have increasingly objected to bearing the risks of nuclear power generation while the economic benefits flow primarily to the capital. The [[Fukushima Anti-Nuclear Movement]], which gained significant momentum after the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] exposed the vulnerability of automated safety systems, has become a major political force in the prefecture.

The [[Yonezawa]]-Okami relationship is defined by cultural and economic exchange. Yonezawa is the center of the [[Kwangju]] cultural heartland, and the Kwangju community of Okami's Tatara ward maintains strong ties to the prefecture through family networks, cultural organizations, and business relationships. The Yonezawa textile industry, which produces the high-quality fabrics used by Okami's fashion houses, is a critical link in the capital's luxury goods supply chain. The [[Yonezawa-Okami Cultural Exchange Program]], established in 1995, funds annual exchanges of artists, musicians, and cultural practitioners between the two cities.


==Demographics==
{{Main|Demographics of Okami}}
Okami's population of over 8.2 million in the special wards and 36.9 million in the metropolitan area makes it the most populous urban area in Okaiken and one of the largest in Adonia. The city's population density of approximately 4,400 per km² in the special wards is among the highest in the nation, though lower than some smaller urban centers.

The city has experienced steady population growth throughout the post-war period, though growth rates have slowed in recent decades as the metropolitan area has matured. The population of the special wards peaked at approximately 8.9 million in 2015 before declining slightly, reflecting broader demographic trends toward suburbanization and an aging population.

