The total fertility rate in the metropolitan area is approximately 1.72 children per woman (2025), below the national average of 1.86 and significantly below replacement level. Fertility rates are lowest in the affluent wards (Aoi ~1.18, Sakura ~1.22) and highest in the working-class wards (Tatara ~2.32, Minami ~2.05), a pattern consistent with the global relationship between income and fertility. The city's population growth is therefore driven primarily by net immigration from the regional prefectures rather than by natural increase.

The infant mortality rate in the metropolitan area is approximately 5.4 per 1,000 live births — below the national average of 6.8 per 1,000, but with significant variation: in Aoi the rate is below 2.1 per 1,000, while in Tatara it exceeds 10.8 per 1,000.

