The Imperial Palace's sacred grounds house the [[Sanshu no Jingi]], the three sacred treasures of the Imperial regalia, making the city the spiritual as well as political center of the nation. Religious observance remains significant in city life, with major festivals drawing millions of participants annually. The [[Okami Summer Festival]], held in January and February, includes hundreds of events throughout the city, culminating in massive fireworks displays over the Hiryū River. The [[Cherry Blossom Festival]] in March and April attracts millions of visitors to the city's parks and gardens.


==Education==
{{Main|Education in Okaiken|Higher education in Okaiken}}
Okami is the academic and scientific center of Okaiken, hosting the highest concentration of research universities, national laboratories, and specialized institutes in the country. Higher education in the capital operates under the national framework overseen by the [[Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology (Okaiken)|Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology]] (MEXT) and the [[National Council for Academic Degrees and University Evaluation]] (NCADUE).

===Universities and Higher Education===
[[File:Okami Teikoku University campus.png|thumb|left|The main campus of Okami Imperial University in Kanda.]]
[[Okami Imperial University]], located in the [[Kanda Ward, Okami|Kanda]] academic district, is Okaiken's oldest and most selective national research university. Founded in 1877, it anchors the '''Imperial Universities Conference''' ({{lang|ja|帝国大学会議}}, ''Teikoku Daigaku Kaigi'') and leads national research output across theoretical physics, law, medicine, and computational sciences. In the western metropolitan municipality of [[Oka, Okami Metropolis|Oka]], the [[University of Okami]] serves as the metropolis's second-ranked public research institution and largest university by enrollment, anchoring the four-kilometre ''Oka Academic Belt'' of pharmaceutical and precision-engineering laboratories.

Other major degree-granting institutions in the capital region include the [[Okami Institute of Technology]] and the [[Satori Development Institute]] in Kanda, which specialize in artificial intelligence, robotics, and semiconductor engineering; [[Keio University]], [[Yuki University]] (in Tamade), [[Hojo University]] (in Tobe), and [[Gakushuin University]] (in Aoi), which anchor the metropolitan private university conferences; and specialized academies including the [[National Defense Academy]], the [[Imperial Music Academy]], and the [[Okami University of the Arts]]. Under Okaiken's ''kenkyū daiichi gakui'' (research-first degree) system, all bachelor's candidates at accredited metropolitan universities must complete and defend an original empirical or theoretical senior thesis registered in the National Academic Thesis Repository prior to graduation, while graduate faculties maintain strict separation between sequential master's ({{lang|ja|修士}}, ''shūshi'') and doctoral ({{lang|ja|博士}}, ''hakase'') candidacy.

===Primary and Secondary Education===
Primary and secondary education in Okami is administered by the Okami Metropolitan Board of Education in coordination with the 20 special ward boards and municipal authorities. Following the national 6–3–3 structure, the metropolitan government operates over 2,000 public elementary and lower secondary schools and approximately 300 upper secondary schools, including designated Super Science High Schools ({{lang|ja|スーパーサイエンスハイスクール}}) partnered with metropolitan research universities.

==Culture==
{{Main|Culture of Okami}}
Okami's cultural influence extends throughout Okaiken and beyond. The city has been the center of Sanese culture for over four centuries, and its arts, cuisine, and fashion trends influence the entire nation. The city's cultural dominance has intensified in the 21st century, as the concentration of media, entertainment, and creative industries in the capital has accelerated the {{Nihongo|"Okami magnet"|大紙磁石|Okami-jishaku}} effect that draws young talent from every region of the archipelago.

===Music===
Okami is the undisputed center of the Sanese music industry. The city's [[O-Pop]] industry, centred on the [[Tamashima]] entertainment district and the [[Aoi]] label corridor, generates over 円1.24 trillion in annual revenue and employs approximately 420,000 workers in the metropolitan area. The [[PYRAMID30]] charts, compiled weekly from sales data, streaming figures, and radio airplay, are the definitive measure of commercial success in Sanese popular music, and an appearance on the PYRAMID30 is considered the essential credential for any artist seeking national recognition.

The major O-Pop labels—including [[Siren Records]], [[Tensei Music]], and [[Crystal Wave Entertainment]]—are all headquartered in the Aoi-Chuo corridor. The industry's production infrastructure, including recording studios, talent agencies, choreography studios, and marketing firms, is concentrated within a 3-kilometre radius that has been termed the {{Nihongo|'Sound District'|音域|On-iki}}. The district's density has created a self-reinforcing ecosystem where producers, songwriters, and performers circulate between labels and projects, creating a distinctive 'Okami sound' that dominates the national charts.

The [[Idol Liberation Act]] of 2016, which ended the restrictive contract system that had governed the idol industry for decades, paradoxically accelerated the concentration of idol culture in Okami. The freed idols, no longer bound to their original agencies, migrated to the capital to establish independent careers, and the resulting proliferation of small labels and independent production companies made Okami the only city with the critical mass of talent, infrastructure, and media access needed to sustain a modern idol career. The [[Okami Idol Festival]], held annually in the Tamashima district, draws over 500,000 attendees and is one of the largest music events in Fosia.

The [[O-Rock]] scene, centred on the live houses of the Tamade and Tama wards, provides an alternative to the polished production of mainstream O-Pop. The [[Tamashima Crossing]] venue has launched dozens of nationally successful rock acts, while the [[Nagashima Broadway]] complex houses a dense cluster of smaller venues catering to niche genres. The [[Sanese Drill]] hip-hop subgenre, which emerged from the Tatara and Minami wards in the late 2010s, has gained national prominence with its distinctive regional sound blending traditional Sanese musical elements with trap production and lyrics addressing urban inequality and working-class identity.

The virtual idol industry—AI-generated performers who exist only as digital avatars—has become a uniquely Sanese cultural export centred on Okami. The most successful virtual idol, {{Nihongo|''Satori-hime''|悟り姫|Awakening Princess}}, generates over 円8 billion in annual merchandise and streaming revenue. The [[Okami Esports Arena]], a 30,000-seat venue opened in 2023, hosts the annual [[Shendan Cup]] tournament and has established the city as the premier competitive gaming destination in the East Fosian sphere.

Classical music maintains a strong presence in the city. The [[Okami Metropolitan Symphony Orchestra]], based at the [[Okami Metropolitan Opera House]], is considered one of the finest orchestras in East Fosia. The [[Imperial Music Academy]], founded in 1892, trains the nation's elite classical musicians, and its graduates dominate the principal chairs of the nation's orchestras.

===Nightlife===
[[File:Okami Golden Gai bars.png|thumb|right|The Golden Gai micro-bars in the Neon District, each seating fewer than ten customers.]]
Okami's nightlife scene is legendary throughout Adonia. The {{Nihongo|'''Neon District'''|ネオン区|Neon-ku}} in Tamashima operates at full intensity from dusk until dawn, with hundreds of bars, nightclubs, live music venues, and restaurants packed into a dense labyrinth of narrow streets and alleyways. The district's irregular street grid—a legacy of its origins as a chaotic labor encampment during the Honda period—defies the orthogonal planning of the other islands, creating a disorienting but thrilling urban environment.

The district is organized into distinct sub-areas catering to different clienteles. The {{Nihongo|''Golden Gai''|黄金街|Ōgon-gai}} is a dense cluster of tiny bars, many seating fewer than ten customers, catering to aficionados of jazz, blues, and experimental music. The {{Nihongo|''Kabukichō''|歌舞伎町}} area hosts larger nightclubs and cabarets, while the {{Nihongo|''Yoshiwara''|吉原}} quarter preserves its centuries-old identity as an entertainment district with high-end restaurants and exclusive clubs. The {{Nihongo|''Electric Street''|電気街|Denki-gai}} corridor is the center of the city's gaming and anime subculture, with multi-story arcades, themed cafés, and merchandise stores operating late into the night.

The [[Okami Nightlife Association]] estimates that the district draws approximately 300,000 visitors on a typical Friday night, with numbers swelling to over 500,000 during festival periods. The district's economic impact is estimated at 円850 billion annually, making it one of the city's most important commercial zones.

===Fashion===
[[File:Okami Shibuya 109 fashion.png|thumb|left|A retail complex in the Tamashima district, the center of youth fashion and street style.]]
Okami is the undisputed fashion capital of Okaiken and one of the most influential style centers in Adonia. The city's fashion industry is organized into distinct districts, each catering to different segments of the market.

The [[Aoi]] luxury corridor, centred on the [[Miyoshi-suji]] shopping street, houses the flagship stores of international luxury brands and the nation's premier domestic designers. The corridor's boutiques cater to the city's elite and the thousands of affluent tourists who visit each year. The [[Ginza]] district, adjacent to Aoi, offers a more accessible luxury experience, with department stores and boutiques targeting the upper-middle-class consumer.

The [[Tamashima]] district in Tama Ward is the center of youth fashion and street style. The ten-story retail complexes around Tamashima Kitaguchi and Omi have launched dozens of fashion trends that have spread throughout Okaiken and beyond, documented by fashion photographers who congregate along the district's pedestrian scrambles.

The [[Hiyori-cho]] and [[Kasuganohara]] quarters in Nagata Ward form the center of alternative fashion and subculture style. The district's [[Takeshita Street]] is famous for its concentration of boutiques catering to distinctive youth subcultures, from the elaborate 'decora' style to the gothic and punk-influenced looks that have become internationally recognized symbols of Sanese fashion creativity.

===Media===
Okami is the center of the Sanese media industry. The nation's major newspapers—including [[The Okami Shimbun]], [[The Sanese Times]], and [[The Daily Yomiuri]]—are headquartered in the central wards. The [[NIBI]] state broadcaster and the major commercial television networks broadcast from studios concentrated in the Minato and Tama wards.

The city's publishing industry, centred on the traditional paper-making districts of Mugishima, remains significant despite the broader global decline in print media. The district's printing presses produce the nation's major newspapers, magazines, and books, maintaining the city's historic identity as 'Big Paper.' The [[Okami International Broadcasting Center]], located in the Minato waterfront development, serves as the primary hub for international news organizations operating in Okaiken.

====The Okaiken Times and the Capital's Mindset====
[[File:Okami Times building.png|thumb|right|The Okaiken Times headquarters in the Nagata government district.]]
[[The Okaiken Times]], founded in 1892 and headquartered in the Nagata government district since 1935, is the nation's newspaper of record and one of the most influential media institutions in Okaiken. With a daily circulation of approximately 2.8 million (print and digital combined), the Times shapes the political and economic discourse of the capital—and by extension, the nation—to a degree that is difficult to overstate.

The Times' editorial philosophy, which has remained remarkably consistent across its 134-year history, reflects the worldview of the Sanese bureaucratic and professional elite: internationalist in outlook, technocratic in temperament, and deeply skeptical of both populist demagoguery and ideological extremism. The paper's famous editorial position—{{Nihongo|''rikushin bunbō''|陸心文房|'land-heart document-chamber'}}—advocates for evidence-based governance, fiscal responsibility, and the maintenance of Okaiken's strategic autonomy in an uncertain geopolitical environment. This worldview, which the paper's critics characterize as "comfortable centrism" and its defenders term "principled pragmatism," has profoundly shaped the self-image of the Okami professional class.

The Times' influence extends beyond its editorial pages. The paper's [[Okami Economic Survey]], published quarterly since 1958, is the most widely cited source of economic data on the metropolitan area and is used by government agencies, corporate strategists, and academic researchers. The Survey's methodology—particularly its classification of the "Okami magnet" effect and its measurement of regional economic concentration—has become the standard framework for understanding the Sanese economy's structural imbalance.

The paper's coverage of the [[Cult of Iron|physical culture movement]] and the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] has been a study in the Times' characteristic ambivalence toward cultural change. While the paper's health and science sections have covered the Initiative's public health benefits extensively, its editorial board has repeatedly expressed concern about the movement's "militaristic undertones" and its potential to undermine the individualistic values that the Times considers essential to Sanese democracy. This tension—between the paper's respect for evidence-based policy and its discomfort with the cultural implications of the fitness movement—mirrors the broader ambivalence of the Okami elite toward the physical culture movement's growing influence.

The [[Okaiken Times Digital Archive]], launched in 2006, provides searchable access to the paper's entire print run and has become an essential research tool for historians, journalists, and policy analysts. The Archive's servers, housed in the paper's Nagata headquarters, are among the most heavily accessed digital resources in the nation.


===Cuisine===
[[File:Okami ramen shop Minami.png|thumb|left|A ramen shop on Ramen Row in the Minami ward.]]
Okami cuisine reflects the city's history as a center of trade and political power. The city is famous for its distinctive style of sushi, developed in the Honda period when fresh fish was delivered to the capital via fast boats from the coast. The [[Tsukiji Outer Market]] remains a major culinary attraction, offering fresh seafood and traditional street foods.

{{Nihongo|'''Okami ramen'''|大紙ラーメン|Okami rāmen}}, a soy-based noodle soup developed in the post-war period, has become one of the city's most iconic dishes. The city's ramen culture is centred on the {{Nihongo|''Ramen Row''|ラーメン横丁|Rāmen yokochō}} in the Minami ward, where dozens of specialized shops compete with distinctive variations on the basic recipe. {{Nihongo|'''Monjayaki'''|もんじゃ焼き}}, a savory pancake similar to okonomiyaki but with a runnier batter, is a specialty of the Tsukishima district.

The city's restaurant scene is among the most diverse in Adonia, with establishments ranging from centuries-old traditional restaurants to cutting-edge molecular gastronomy. The [[Adonia Guide]] has awarded Okami by far the most Rosalin stars of any city in the world, reflecting both the quality of its traditional cuisine and its embrace of international culinary innovation.

===Festivals===
Okami hosts hundreds of festivals throughout the year, drawing participants and spectators from across the nation. The [[Okami Summer Festival]], held in January and February, includes hundreds of events throughout the city, culminating in massive fireworks displays over the Hiryū River. The [[Cherry Blossom Festival]] in March and April attracts millions of visitors to the city's parks and gardens. The [[Festival of the Lanterns]], held in the historic Tamashima district each autumn, preserves traditions dating to the Honda period with traditional music, dance, and elaborate lantern displays that illuminate the narrow streets of the entertainment district.

===Museums and Cultural Institutions===
[[File:Okami National Museum.png|thumb|right|The Okami National Museum, housing the world's largest collection of Sanese art and artifacts.]]
Okami is home to the nation's premier museums and cultural institutions. The [[Okami National Museum]] houses the world's largest collection of Sanese art and artifacts, spanning from prehistoric times to the present. The [[National Museum of Modern Art]] showcases the development of modern Sanese art. The [[Okami Metropolitan Art Museum]] hosts major international exhibitions.

The city's performing arts scene is centered on the [[Okami Metropolitan Opera House]] and the [[National Theater of Okaiken]]. Traditional performing arts, including ''kabuki'', ''noh'', and ''bunraku'', are performed at historic venues including the [[Kabuki-za Theater]] in the Ginza district. The [[Okami International Film Festival]], held each October, draws filmmakers and critics from around the world.


===Sports===
{{Main|Sport in Okaiken|Cult of Iron}}
[[File:X-League game action.jpg|thumb|right|An X-League gridiron football match at the Tatara Sports Complex.]]
Okami's sports culture reflects the national passion for both traditional and modern athletic pursuits, and the city is the epicentre of the {{nihongo|[[physical culture movement]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} that has become one of the defining features of modern Sanese identity.

The city is home to the [[Okami Direwolves]] of the [[X-League]], one of the most storied franchises in Sanese professional gridiron football, and the [[Okami Giants]], one of the nation's most popular professional baseball teams. The [[Tatara Sports Complex]]—the administrative headquarters of the [[National Iron Body Initiative]]—has produced numerous national and international champions in boxing, wrestling, bodybuilding, and mixed martial arts, and the complex's annual [[Mr. Adonia]] bodybuilding championship is the premier event in the Fosian bodybuilding calendar.

The physical culture movement's influence on Okami's sports culture is pervasive. The [[Tatara]] ward's {{nihongo|"Tatara Circuit"|多々羅回廊|Tatara-kairō}}—a cluster of premium gyms, supplement shops, and recovery spas—has become a fitness pilgrimage destination, and the ward's distinctive {{nihongo|"guardian physique"|守護体格|Shugo-taikaku}} aesthetic has become the dominant body ideal for young men across the city. The [[Okami Marathon]], held each February, attracts over 40,000 runners and is the largest participation sporting event in Okaiken.

[[File:Okami Kokugikan sumo.png|thumb|left|The Kokugikan Arena during a grand sumo tournament.]]
The sport of ''sumo'' is closely associated with Okami, with the [[Kokugikan Arena]] in the Ryogoku district hosting three of the six annual grand tournaments. The [[Kenka-Jutsu]] full-contact martial arts circuit holds its national championship at the Tatara Sports Complex each December.

==Transportation==
{{main|Urban transport in Okami|Okami Metro}}
Okami is the central logistical hub of Okaiken's domestic and international transit networks. The Greater Okami Metropolitan Area possesses one of the most extensive urban rail networks in Adonia, comprising 54 rail lines operated by [[Okaiken Railways]] (OR Harujima), [[Okami Metro]], and 14 private railway operators across 1,129 operator stations (928 station complexes), handling approximately 23.83 million daily passenger gate entries.

===Okami Metro===
[[File:Okami-Metro Series4000-01.png|thumb|right|A Series 4000 train on the Okami Metro network.]]
The [[Okami Metro]] is the primary rapid transit system serving the 20 special wards and surrounding municipalities of [[Okami Metropolis]]. Originally opened on June 15, 1935, as the Okami Municipal Subway and privatized on April 1, 2018, under [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]], the network comprises 13 lines (12 heavy rapid transit lines and one automated guideway transit line, the [[Nankō Port Town Line]]) spanning {{cvt|512.0|km|mi|abbr=on}} and serving 413 stations. The system handles approximately 4.98 million unlinked daily gate entries and 20,308,903 cumulative daily line boardings (FY2025), and is deeply integrated with the [[Satori System|Satori AI]] passenger-flow and headway optimization suite.

