The Metro's integration with the Satori system, implemented following the 2018 privatization, enables real-time optimization of train frequencies based on passenger demand, feeding telemetry from automated platform screen doors and gate sensors across all 413 stations. However, the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of 2026 exposed the vulnerabilities of centralized automation when a network-wide outage disrupted CBTC signalling across the subway lines during the morning rush hour, prompting mandatory analogue fallback protocols under the [[Digital Sovereignty Act]].

===Rail Transportation===
{{main|Okaiken Railways|Harujima Shinkansen|List of busiest stations in Okami}}
[[File:Okami Chuo Station.png|thumb|right|Okami Chūō Station, the nation's busiest rail hub.]]
[[Okami Chuo Station|Okami Chūō Station]] in Chiyoda Ward is the busiest railway station complex in Okaiken, handling 1,376,201 combined daily passengers (502.3 million annually) across 16 island platforms and 32 tracks served by [[Okaiken Railways]] (OR Harujima), [[Okami Metro]] (Miyoshi, Chūō, and Shitayanagi lines), the [[Fukukyu Fukushima Line]], and the [[Matsubara Electric Railway Senri Line]]. Other major rail hubs in the city include [[Minami Okami Station]] (974,970 daily passengers), the private-railway terminal complex at [[Otemachi Station|Otemachi]] (547,811 daily passengers across the Miyoshi, Nanboku, and Shitayanagi Metro lines and the [[Akitetsu Akita Line]], [[Sanshi Main Line]], [[Yometsu Main Line]], and Matsubara Senri Line), [[Hozumi-Oiwake Station|Hozumi-Oiwake]] (505,067 daily passengers), and [[Kamiyacho Station|Kamiya-chō]] (470,506 daily passengers).

High-speed intercity rail service is provided by the [[Harujima Shinkansen]], operated by OR along a dedicated standard-gauge corridor connecting [[Shinagawa]] in the north to [[Toyozawa]] in the south via [[Okami Chuo Station|Okami Chūō]], [[Minami Okami Station|Minami-Okami]], and [[Okami-Tobe Station|Okami-Tobe]]:

