Conventional regional and commuter rail is operated by two distinct networks: [[Okaiken Railways]] (OR Harujima), which operates 19 narrow-gauge ({{RailGauge|1067mm}}) lines across 314 stations in the metropolitan area (carrying 11.40 million daily passengers) including the [[OR Main Line]], the 16-station circumferential [[OR Okami Loop Line]], the [[OR Iwashi Line]], the [[OR Fukushima Line]], the [[OR Okazaki Line]], and the [[OR Minato Line]]; and 14 private railway operators ({{nihongo|''ōtemetsu''|大手私鉄}}) operating 22 primarily standard-gauge ({{RailGauge|sg}}) lines across 402 stations (carrying 6.95 million daily passengers), led by [[Akitetsu]], [[Fukukyu]], [[Matsubara Electric Railway]], [[Okaden]], [[Sanshi]], [[Tokuden]], [[Toukai]], [[Sugitetsu]], [[Yometsu]], [[Yometetsu]], and [[Gomyo Railway]].

===The Miyoshi Line===
{{main|Miyoshi Line}}
[[File:Okami Metro Miyoshi line symbol.svg|thumb|right|100px|The Miyoshi Line symbol (Line 1 / M).]]
The '''[[Miyoshi Line]]''' ({{lang|ja|三好線}}, ''Miyoshi-sen''; '''Line 1 / M''', colored lavender) is the oldest and most historically significant underground rapid transit line in the Okami metropolitan area. Running {{cvt|54.5|km|mi|abbr=on}} ({{cvt|52.94|km|mi|abbr=on}} in GIS route alignment) across 43 stations from [[Yuyama Chuo]] ({{OMSN|M|01}}) in the northern suburb of Yuyama to [[Okada]] ({{OMSN|M|43}}) in the southern municipality of Masaki, the line serves as the primary north–south subterranean spine beneath Miyoshi Boulevard across the five delta islands, handling 2,227,897 daily passenger boardings.

====History and Role as Okami's North–South Spine====
The Miyoshi Line was conceived in the late 1920s by the Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau after surface streetcar speeds along Miyoshi Boulevard dropped below {{cvt|10|km/h|mph|abbr=on}} due to severe urban congestion. Construction commenced in 1930, and the initial segment between [[Hozumi-Oiwake]] ({{OMSN|M|15}}) and [[Okami Chuo Station|Okami Chūō]] ({{OMSN|M|20}}) opened on June 15, 1935, as the first publicly operated subway line in Okaiken. Built to {{RailGauge|sg|allk=on}} standard gauge with {{1,500 V DC}} overhead catenary, the line was engineered for high-capacity urban trunk service.

Following wartime interruptions during the [[Great Adonian War]], when completed subway bores were used as civil air-raid shelters, the Miyoshi Line was extended southward across the delta waterways via underwater shield tunnels to [[Tamashima]] and [[Tama Center]] ({{OMSN|M|26}}) between 1964 and 1972, and subsequently through [[Tamade]], [[Rokumanji]], [[Minato]], and [[Tobe]] before reaching its southern terminus at [[Okada]] in [[Masaki]] in 2005. Operating 8-car '''Type B''' formations of [[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]] and flagship [[Okami Metro 4000 series|4000 series]] rolling stock at headways as short as 90 seconds, the Miyoshi Line intersects nearly every radial and cross-town corridor in the capital.

