===Air Transportation===
[[File:Okami Tobu International Airport.png|thumb|right|Okami-Tobu International Airport on reclaimed land in the Tobu waterfront district.]]
[[Okami-Tobu International Airport]], located on reclaimed land in the Mitsu-Minato waterfront district, is the primary international aviation gateway to Okaiken, handling approximately 45 million passengers annually and serving as the main hub for [[Okaiken Air]] and [[Sanesair]]. The airport's Terminal 1 and Terminal 2 are directly served by the Okami Metro [[Imaichi Line]] ({{OMSN|I|01}} and {{OMSN|I|02}}) and the [[OR Okami-Tobu Line]] (which operates the ''Tobu Express'' along the OR Main Line through Okami Chūō).

Commercial domestic and regional flights also utilize [[Tokuyama Hirao Airport]] in the southern metropolitan corridor, which is connected to central Okami by the [[Tokuden Airport Express Line]], the OR Tokuyama Hirao Airport Line, and the multi-operator ''Hirao Express'' through-service via the Toukai–Imaichi Rail Link.

===Road Transportation===
Okami is connected to the national [[expressway]] network via several major routes. The [[Okami Expressway]] circles the city center, while the [[Harujima Expressway]] connects the capital with the northern prefectures. The [[Tōkaidō Expressway]] follows the historic coastal route to the southern cities. Traffic congestion remains a significant challenge despite extensive infrastructure development, and the metropolitan government has implemented congestion pricing in the central wards to encourage public transportation use.

The city's road network is complicated by the five-island geography, with over 120 bridges connecting the islands. The major bridges—including the [[Kōryū Bridge]] (connecting Tenkyō-jima and Tamashima), the [[Hiryū Bridge]] (connecting Mugishima and Nagashima), and the [[Tatara Bridge]] (connecting Ryūbi-jima to the mainland)—are critical transportation links that carry both vehicular and pedestrian traffic.

===Port Facilities===
[[File:Okami Port container facilities.png|thumb|left|The automated container port facilities on Ryūbi-jima.]]
The [[Port of Okami]] is one of the nation's largest commercial ports, handling container traffic, bulk cargo, and passenger ferries. The port facilities, concentrated on the eastern shore of Ryūbi-jima, have been extensively modernized since the post-war reconstruction and now include some of the most automated cargo handling facilities in Adonia. The port handles approximately 40% of the nation's maritime cargo by value and serves as a major hub for ferry services to the [[Inland Mitoyagi Islands]] and the coastal cities of Harujima.

The [[Okami Ferry Terminal]], located on the northern shore of Ryūbi-jima, provides regular passenger services to [[Tokuyama]], [[Shinagawa]], and the [[Inland Mitoyagi Islands]]. The terminal's distinctive modernist design, completed in 1985, has become an architectural landmark in its own right.


==International Relations==
===Diplomatic Missions===
As the national capital, Okami hosts the embassies and consulates of all nations maintaining diplomatic relations with Okaiken. The [[Kasumigaseki Diplomatic District]] houses the majority of foreign embassies, while honorary consulates are located throughout the metropolitan area. The city also hosts the regional headquarters of numerous international organizations.

===Sister Cities===
[[File:Okami sister cities map.svg|thumb|right|Okami's sister city relationships around the world.]]
Okami maintains sister city relationships with major cities around the world, promoting cultural exchange and economic cooperation. Major sister city relationships include:
* [[New Valencia]], [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] (since 1960)
* [[Akyatan]], [[Dagit]] (since 1975)
* [[Yomitan]], [[Yanbaru]] (since 1985)
* [[Fuzhou]], [[Kingdom of Zong]] (since 1992)

==Notable People==
{{Main|List of people from Okami}}
Okami has been home to numerous significant figures throughout Okaiken's history. Historical figures include the Honda shoguns who ruled from the city for over 250 years, and the statesmen of the Saito Restoration who transformed Okaiken into a modern nation. The city has produced countless artists, writers, and intellectuals who have shaped Sanese culture.

Notable modern figures include [[Honda Shigetsuma]], the current Prime Minister of Okaiken; [[Aono Michifumi]], the current Governor of Okami; numerous business leaders who head the nation's largest corporations; and artists and entertainers who have achieved international recognition.

